+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+To show progress while a file is read, use the :func:`~rich.progress.open` function. It takes a path and the same mode, buffering, encoding, errors and newline arguments as the builtin ``open``. The default total is the size of the file in bytes, so the bar advances as the file is read. Use it as a context manager, which starts the display on entry and stops it on exit::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.json") as file:
+        text = file.read()
+
+The default mode is ``'r'``, which returns text as a ``str``. Pass ``'rb'`` as the mode to read ``bytes`` instead::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("image.png", "rb") as file:
+        data = file.read()
+
+If you already have a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, use its :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` method. It adds a task, or updates an existing one with ``task_id``, and returns the file without starting the display. Start the display yourself, and close the file when you have finished::
+
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    progress = Progress()
+    file = progress.open("data.json", description="Reading data.json")
+    with progress:
+        text = file.read()
+    file.close()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file object, use the :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` function. It returns a binary reader that advances the progress as bytes are read. The ``total`` argument is required, as the size of a file-like object cannot be found. Pass the size of the file in bytes::
+
+    import os
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    path = "data.bin"
+    with open(path, "rb") as file:
+        with wrap_file(file, total=os.path.getsize(path)) as reader:
+            data = reader.read()
+
+The reader does not close the file you wrap, so the file remains open after the block. Only binary file objects are supported.
+
+With a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, use :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` instead. It takes an optional ``task_id``. If the task has a total, you can leave out the ``total`` argument::
+
+    import os
+    from rich.progress import Progress
+
+    path = "data.bin"
+    progress = Progress()
+    task_id = progress.add_task("Reading data.bin", total=os.path.getsize(path))
+    with progress:
+        with open(path, "rb") as file:
+            reader = progress.wrap_file(file, task_id=task_id)
+            data = reader.read()
+
